`ErrorBoundary.tsx` is ready. Here's a summary of what it does:

**API**
| Prop | Type | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `children` | `ReactNode` | The subtree to guard |
| `fallback` | `(error, retry) => ReactNode` | Optional custom fallback UI |

**Behaviour**
- `getDerivedStateFromError` — stores the caught error in state, triggering the fallback render.
- `componentDidCatch` — logs the error and React component stack to `console.error`.
- `retry()` — resets state back to `null`, which re-mounts the children so they get a fresh attempt.

**Usage**

```tsx
// Default fallback
<ErrorBoundary>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>

// Custom fallback
<ErrorBoundary
  fallback={(error, retry) => (
    <div>
      <p>Oops: {error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={retry}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  )}
>
  <MyComponent />
</ErrorBoundary>
```

> Note: Error boundaries only catch errors during **rendering**, lifecycle methods, and constructors of class components — not inside event handlers (use `try/catch` there) or async code.
